      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The Sunday Rock Legacy Project, founded in 2012, researches and celebrates Colton’s history every year.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.townofcolton.com/local-history--highlights.html",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Colton’s historic center is flanked by the Raquette River, which drops 40 feet through the hamlet before heading north to the St. Lawrence River.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Colton_(CDP)%2C_New_York",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "New York State Route 56 cuts through Colton, linking the hamlet to Potsdam 9 miles north and to the Adirondacks 23 miles southeast.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        18
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Colton_(CDP)%2C_New_York",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "route_only"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The Colton Historical Society, Colton‑Pierrepont Central School, and Grasse River Players collaborate each year on projects that blend history, theater, and education.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4,
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.townofcolton.com/local-history--highlights.html",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Colton is the only community in the state that has a single rock listed on the National Register of Historic Places.",
